[RUNTIME] /src/code_forge/eval/runner.py:476-476 - Lifecycle/Side-effect risks: Increasing the subprocess timeout limit from 300s to 1800s (30 minutes) alongside `capture_output=True` risks causing an Out-Of-Memory (OOM) fatal event in the parent Python process if a runaway child process spams stdout/stderr, as output is buffered completely in RAM. Additionally, this longer lifespan significantly increases the risk that an external CI job timeout or global hypervisor scheduler abruptly SIGKILLs the whole runner before this local timeout even fires, thereby masking the error and dropping logs. Finally, `subprocess.run` timeout handling only terminates the direct child; if `code-forge` delegates to other binaries, they may be left as orphaned zombie processes leaking resources.

Smoke test: Execute the runner with a mocked `code-forge review` script that deeply nests a child process and loops infinite stdout printing. Configure a short `FORGE_EVAL_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_S=2`. Verify it catches the TimeoutExpired gracefully, does not OOM the parent process, and successfully reaps all descendant processes.

Concurrent/Stateful dependencies: Interacts directly with the OS's process lifecycle/memory manager and external CI supervisor job boundaries (which can independently drop or interrupt the runner without warning). Further, it indirectly depends on remote network backends doing the LLM review, which are the main cause for hangs. Gated by the environment variable flag: `FORGE_EVAL_REVIEW_TIMEOUT_S`. All possible values at call time: None/Unset (defaults to 1800), unparseable strings (e.g. "invalid", catches ValueError/TypeError and defaults to 1800), integers <= 0 (e.g. "0" or "-10", defaults to 1800), and valid integers > 0 (successfully overrides the timeout in seconds).
